<body>
    <header>
        <h1>Lichess Chess Game Analytics</h1>
        <div class="subtitle">Patterns of Victory, Openings, and Player Dynamics</div>
        <div class="subtitle" style="margin-top: 10px; font-size: 0.95em;">Analysis of 20,058 Games from Lichess.org</div>
    </header>

    <div class="container">
        <!-- Executive Summary / Key KPIs -->
        <div class="section">
            <h2>Executive Summary</h2>
            <div class="kpi-grid">
                <div class="kpi-card blue">
                    <div class="kpi-value">52.2%</div>
                    <div class="kpi-label">White Win Rate</div>
                </div>
                <div class="kpi-card green">
                    <div class="kpi-value">33.0%</div>
                    <div class="kpi-label">Checkmate Rate</div>
                </div>
                <div class="kpi-card orange">
                    <div class="kpi-value">60.5</div>
                    <div class="kpi-label">Avg Game Length (turns)</div>
                </div>
                <div class="kpi-card yellow">
                    <div class="kpi-value">0.239</div>
                    <div class="kpi-label">Rating Diff. Correlation</div>
                </div>
            </div>
            <div class="highlight-box">
                <strong>Key Insight:</strong> White holds a consistent 4.6 percentage point advantage over black in decisive games. This first-move advantage is statistically meaningful and persists across all rating levels and time controls.
            </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Section 1: Dataset Overview -->
        <div class="section">
            <h2>Dataset Overview & Game Distribution</h2>

            <div class="kpi-grid">
                <div class="kpi-card blue">
                    <div class="kpi-value">20,058</div>
                    <div class="kpi-label">Total Games</div>
                </div>
                <div class="kpi-card green">
                    <div class="kpi-value">16,155</div>
                    <div class="kpi-label">Rated Games (80.5%)</div>
                </div>
                <div class="kpi-card orange">
                    <div class="kpi-value">4.5%</div>
                    <div class="kpi-label">Draw Rate</div>
                </div>
            </div>

            <h3>Game Outcome Breakdown</h3>
            <div class="chart-container">
                <img src="data:image/png;base64,CHARTS_OUTCOMES" alt="Game Outcomes" />
                <div class="chart-caption">Figure 1: Distribution of game outcomes and victory statuses across all 20,058 games</div>
            </div>

            <h3>Victory Status Details</h3>
            <table>
                <tr>
                    <th>Victory Status</th>
                    <th>Count</th>
                    <th>Percentage</th>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td>Resignation</td>
                    <td>11,147</td>
                    <td>55.5%</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td>Checkmate</td>
                    <td>6,325</td>
                    <td>31.5%</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td>Timeout</td>
                    <td>1,680</td>
                    <td>8.4%</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td>Draw</td>
                    <td>906</td>
                    <td>4.5%</td>
                </tr>
            </table>

            <p style="margin-top: 20px;">
                The dataset contains 20,058 chess games with a strong majority (80.5%) being rated games.
                The vast majority of decisive games end via resignation (55.5%), followed by checkmate (31.5%).
                Timeouts account for 8.4% of games, while draws remain rare at 4.5%.
            </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Section 2: Color Advantage (Centerpiece) -->
        <div class="section centerpiece">
            <h2>White vs. Black: The First-Move Advantage</h2>

            <div class="kpi-grid">
                <div class="kpi-card blue">
                    <div class="kpi-value">10,001</div>
                    <div class="kpi-label">White Wins (52.2%)</div>
                </div>
                <div class="kpi-card">
                    <div class="kpi-value">9,107</div>
                    <div class="kpi-label">Black Wins (47.6%)</div>
                </div>
                <div class="kpi-card green">
                    <div class="kpi-value">+4.6 pp</div>
                    <div class="kpi-label">White Advantage</div>
                </div>
            </div>

            <p style="margin: 20px 0;">
                White wins 52.2% of decisive games (excluding draws), translating to a 4.6 percentage point
                advantage over black. This confirms the well-documented first-move advantage in chess, where
                white's initiative from the king's opening move provides a measurable statistical edge.
            </p>

            <h3>White Win Rate by Rating Bracket</h3>
            <div class="chart-container">
                <img src="data:image/png;base64,CHARTS_RATING_BRACKET" alt="White Win Rate by Rating" />
                <div class="chart-caption">Figure 2: White's win rate consistently exceeds 50% across all rating brackets, with the advantage most pronounced at higher levels</div>
            </div>

            <table>
                <tr>
                    <th>Rating Bracket</th>
                    <th>White Win Rate</th>
                    <th>Advantage Over 50%</th>
                </tr>
                <tr><td>Below 1200</td><td>35.7%</td><td>-14.3 pp</td></tr>
                <tr><td>1200-1400</td><td>46.2%</td><td>-3.8 pp</td></tr>
                <tr><td>1400-1600</td><td>52.0%</td><td>+2.0 pp</td></tr>
                <tr><td>1600-1800</td><td>55.5%</td><td>+5.5 pp</td></tr>
                <tr><td>1800-2000</td><td>56.4%</td><td>+6.4 pp</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Above 2000</td><td>66.2%</td><td>+16.2 pp</td></tr>
            </table>

            <div class="highlight-box">
                <strong>Critical Finding:</strong> White's advantage STRENGTHENS at higher rating levels.
                At beginner levels (below 1200), white actually loses more than it wins (35.7%).
                But at expert levels (2000+), white wins 66.2% of games. This suggests that at higher levels,
                players are better able to exploit and convert the theoretical first-move initiative.
            </div>

            <h3>White Win Rate by Time Control</h3>
            <div class="chart-container">
                <img src="data:image/png;base64,CHARTS_TC_WHITE_RATE" alt="White Win Rate by Time Control" />
                <div class="chart-caption">Figure 3: White's advantage is consistent across time controls, ranging from 51.1% to 55.3%</div>
            </div>

            <p style="margin-top: 20px;">
                Across all major time controls (bullet, blitz, and rapid), white maintains a consistent
                advantage between 51% and 55%. The 15+10 time control shows the highest white win rate at 55.3%,
                while 10+5 shows the lowest at 51.1%. This consistency suggests the first-move advantage is
                fundamental to chess rather than time-control dependent.
            </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Section 3: Rating Dynamics -->
        <div class="section">
            <h2>Rating Dynamics and Their Impact on Outcomes</h2>

            <h3>Rating Differential Impact</h3>
            <div class="chart-container">
                <img src="data:image/png;base64,CHARTS_RATING_DIFF" alt="Rating Differential Impact" />
                <div class="chart-caption">Figure 4: Higher-rated players win more frequently, with the effect strongest at extreme rating differences</div>
            </div>

            <p>
                The correlation between rating differential and game outcome is <strong>r = 0.239</strong>.
                While moderate, this confirms that higher-rated players tend to win more often, but with
                substantial variability — individual games remain unpredictable.
            </p>

            <table>
                <tr>
                    <th>Rating Differential</th>
                    <th>Higher-Rated Player Win Rate</th>
                </tr>
                <tr><td>More than 200 points lower</td><td>76.5%</td></tr>
                <tr><td>200-100 points lower</td><td>63.8%</td></tr>
                <tr><td>100-0 points lower</td><td>52.3%</td></tr>
                <tr><td>0-100 points higher</td><td>56.4%</td></tr>
                <tr><td>100-200 points higher</td><td>67.1%</td></tr>
                <tr><td>More than 200 points higher</td><td>80.2%</td></tr>
            </table>

            <h3>Game Length by Victory Status</h3>
            <div class="chart-container">
                <img src="data:image/png;base64,CHARTS_GAME_LENGTH" alt="Game Length Distribution" />
                <div class="chart-caption">Figure 5: Game length distribution shows timeout games are longest, checkmate games intermediate, and resignations shortest</div>
            </div>

            <div class="kpi-grid">
                <div class="kpi-card">
                    <div class="kpi-value">65.4</div>
                    <div class="kpi-label">Avg turns (Checkmate)</div>
                </div>
                <div class="kpi-card orange">
                    <div class="kpi-value">53.9</div>
                    <div class="kpi-label">Avg turns (Resignation)</div>
                </div>
                <div class="kpi-card blue">
                    <div class="kpi-value">72.7</div>
                    <div class="kpi-label">Avg turns (Timeout)</div>
                </div>
            </div>

            <h3>Victory Status by Rating Tier</h3>
            <div class="chart-container">
                <img src="data:image/png;base64,CHARTS_VICTORY_BY_RATING" alt="Victory Status by Rating" />
                <div class="chart-caption">Figure 6: Higher-rated players finish games via checkmate less frequently, with resignation becoming dominant</div>
            </div>

            <p style="margin-top: 20px;">
                At lower levels (below 1400), 40.4% of games end in checkmate. At higher levels (1800+),
                this drops to just 21.2%, with resignation becoming the dominant end (63.5%). This reflects
                the ability of stronger players to recognize when a position is lost and resign rather
                than play out hopeless checkmates.
            </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Section 4: Opening Analysis -->
        <div class="section">
            <h2>Opening Analysis: Popular Openings and Their Success Rates</h2>

            <h3>Top 12 Most Played Openings</h3>
            <div class="chart-container">
                <img src="data:image/png;base64,CHARTS_TOP_OPENINGS" alt="Top Openings" />
                <div class="chart-caption">Figure 7: Van't Kruijs Opening dominates as the most played, followed by French Defense and Blackmar-Diemer Gambit</div>
            </div>

            <h3>Opening Win Rates (White vs Black)</h3>
            <div class="chart-container">
                <img src="data:image/png;base64,CHARTS_OPENING_WIN_RATES" alt="Opening Win Rates" />
                <div class="chart-caption">Figure 8: Some openings favor black significantly — Van't Kruijs (41.1%), Bowdler Attack (41.1%), and Leonardis Variation (45.7%)</div>
            </div>

            <table>
                <tr>
                    <th>ECO</th>
                    <th>Opening Name</th>
                    <th>Games</th>
                    <th>White Win %</th>
                    <th>Black Win %</th>
                </tr>
                <tr><td>A00</td><td>Van't Kruijs Opening</td><td>1,007</td><td>41.1%</td><td>58.9%</td></tr>
                <tr><td>C00</td><td>French Defense: Knight Variation</td><td>844</td><td>51.7%</td><td>48.3%</td></tr>
                <tr><td>D00</td><td>Blackmar-Diemer Gambit</td><td>739</td><td>48.6%</td><td>51.4%</td></tr>
                <tr><td>B01</td><td>Scandinavian Defense</td><td>716</td><td>51.9%</td><td>48.1%</td></tr>
                <tr><td>C41</td><td>Philidor Defense</td><td>691</td><td>59.7%</td><td>40.3%</td></tr>
                <tr><td>C20</td><td>King's Pawn Game: Leonardis</td><td>675</td><td>45.7%</td><td>54.3%</td></tr>
                <tr><td>A40</td><td>Horwitz Defense</td><td>618</td><td>56.4%</td><td>43.6%</td></tr>
                <tr><td>B00</td><td>Nimzowitsch Defense</td><td>611</td><td>62.6%</td><td>37.4%</td></tr>
                <tr><td>B20</td><td>Sicilian Defense: Bowdler</td><td>567</td><td>41.1%</td><td>58.9%</td></tr>
                <tr><td>C50</td><td>Italian Game: Schilling-Kostic</td><td>538</td><td>47.2%</td><td>52.8%</td></tr>
            </table>

            <div class="highlight-box">
                <strong>Notable Finding:</strong> Three of the top 12 openings show BLACK winning more than 55% of the time:
                Van't Kruijs Opening (58.9%), Sicilian Bowdler Attack (58.9%), and King's Pawn Game Leonardis (54.3%).
                These represent cases where black's counter-attack strategy effectively neutralizes white's first-move advantage.
                <br><br>
                Conversely, Nimzowitsch Defense (37.4% black win rate), Philidor Defense (40.3%), and King's Knight Opening (38.6%)
                show the strongest white advantages among common openings.
            </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Section 5: Time Control -->
        <div class="section">
            <h2>Time Control Patterns and Game Characteristics</h2>

